b.io/post/2025-12-17-compositional-mle/</guid><description>An R package where optimization solvers are first-class functions that compose through chaining, racing, and restarts.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-17-compositional-mle/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Sicp</media:title></media:content><category>r-package</category><category>statistics</category><category>mle</category><category>optimization</category><category>sicp</category><category>functional-programming</category><category>dsl</category><category>api-design</category></item><item><title>Rerum: Pattern Matching and Term Rewriting in Python</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-16-rerum/</link><pubDate>Tue, 16 Dec 2025 06:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-16-rerum/</guid><description>A Python library for symbolic computation with a readable DSL, pattern matching, and a security model that separates rules from computation.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-12-16-rerum/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Sicp</media:title></media:content><category>symbolic-computation</category><category>term-rewriting</category><category>pattern-matching</category><category>dsl</category><category>python</category><category>computer-algebra</category><category>functional-programming</category><category>sicp</category></item><item><title>chop: When Every Command Returns the Same Kind of Thing</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-07-15-chop/</link><pubDate>Tue, 15 Jul 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-07-15-chop/</guid><description>27 image commands, one constraint: read JSON, write JSON. The closure property as a generative design principle.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2025-07-15-chop/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Sicp</media:title></media:content><category>sicp</category><category>dsl</category><category>unix-philosophy</category><category>python</category><category>image-processing</category><category>closure-property</category><category>lazy-evaluation</category></item><item><title>jsonl-algebra: Relational Algebra for Nested JSON</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-12-jsonl-algebra/</link><pubDate>Wed, 18 Dec 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-12-jsonl-algebra/</guid><description>A command-line implementation of relational algebra for JSONL data with full support for nested structures, schema inference, and composable pipelines.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-12-jsonl-algebra/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Sicp</media:title></media:content><category>Python</category><category>JSON</category><category>relational-algebra</category><category>ETL</category><category>data-processing</category><category>Unix-philosophy</category><category>command-line</category><category>sicp</category></item><item><title>Apertures: A Language with Holes</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-04-01-aperture/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Apr 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-04-01-aperture/</guid><description>When the problem is coordinating computation across parties who can&amp;#39;t share data, the SICP move is to build a language for it. Apertures adds one primitive — holes — to a Lisp, and gets pausable, resumable evaluation for free.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-04-01-aperture/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Sicp</media:title></media:content><category>sicp</category><category>dsl</category><category>metalinguistic-abstraction</category><category>partial-evaluation</category><category>coordination</category><category>programming-languages</category></item><item><title>Accumux: Compositional Online Statistical Reductions in C++</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-01-accumux/</link><pubDate>Fri, 01 Mar 2024 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-01-accumux/</guid><description>A C++20 library for composing online statistical accumulators with numerically stable algorithms and algebraic composition.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-03-01-accumux/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Sicp</media:title></media:content><category>C++</category><category>statistics</category><category>numerical-computing</category><category>accumulators</category><category>functional-programming</category><category>header-only</category><category>sicp</category></item><item><title>algebraic.dist: Distributions as Algebraic Objects in R</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-05-01-algebraic.dist/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Feb 2021 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-05-01-algebraic.dist/</guid><description>An R package that treats probability distributions as algebraic objects. They compose through standard operations. The algebra preserves distributional structure.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2024-05-01-algebraic.dist/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Sicp</media:title></media:content><category>statistics</category><category>probability</category><category>distributions</category><category>R</category><category>functional programming</category><category>algebra</category><category>sicp</category></item><item><title>How Iterators Give You N+M Instead of NxM</title><link>https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-11-iterators-composition/</link><pubDate>Fri, 15 Nov 2019 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><dc:creator>Alex Towell</dc:creator><guid isPermaLink="true">https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-11-iterators-composition/</guid><description>Iterators reduce the NxM algorithm-container problem to N+M by interposing an abstraction layer, following Stepanov&amp;#39;s generic programming approach.</description><media:content url="https://queelius.github.io/post/2019-11-iterators-composition/card.jpg" medium="image"><media:title type="plain">Sicp</media:title></media:content><category>c++</category><category>generic-programming</category><category>stepanov</category><category>iterators</category><category>sicp</category></item></channel></rss>